<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yazdegerd II</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">r. 438–457</span> · <a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Yazdegerd II was king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> from 438 to 457. He fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantines</a> briefly and campaigned for years against the Kidarites and other Hunnic peoples on his north-eastern frontier. His attempt to impose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> on the Christian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberians">Iberians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> provoked the rising crushed at Avarayr in 451.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yazdegerd-ii.jpg" alt="Yazdegerd II" width="320" height="319" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group; [1] · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YazdegerdIICroppedCoinHistoryofIran.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Yazdegerd II succeeded his father, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahram-v">Bahram V</a>, in 438. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> had agreed in a treaty of 387 to share the defence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> against nomadic attacks, and paid roughly 500 pounds of gold at irregular intervals, which they saw as a subsidy and the Iranians as tribute. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodosius-ii">Theodosius II</a> declined to continue, Yazdegerd declared war, with little success for either side. Vandal attacks on the Roman south led Theodosius to send Anatolius to the Sasanian camp, and in 440 both empires promised not to build new fortifications in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>.</p><p>In the east the Kidarites and other groups known as Iranian Huns had long pressed the empire, and had forced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a> to pay tribute, which Yazdegerd refused. In 450 he led an expedition deep into Kidarite territory, taking forts, cities, captives and wealth. In 453 he moved his court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nishapur">Nishapur</a> and left his minister Mihr Narseh in charge of the empire. His forces first suffered a severe defeat, and the war continued for many years. He fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damghan">Damghan</a> as a border post.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Armenian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syriac">Syriac</a> sources portray Yazdegerd as a religious fanatic, while Arabic and Persian sources show a pious king who clashed with the aristocracy. Persecution of Christians began in 446 with the nobles of Karkh and spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and the Armenian rebellion led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vardan-mamikonian">Vardan Mamikonian</a> was defeated and Vardan killed at Avarayr on 2 June 451, with heavy Sasanian losses. Afterwards religious freedom was allowed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were persecuted too, reportedly with a ban on open observance of the Sabbath. Modern historians such as Eberhard Sauer and Scott McDonough doubt that he was a zealot, and McDonough suggests <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrianism</a> may have been a test of personal loyalty.</p><p>He was the first Sasanian ruler to take the title kay, linking the dynasty to the mythical Kayanians of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avesta">Avesta</a>, and his coins read mazdesn bay kay. In the 440s he had a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudbrick">mudbrick</a> defensive system built at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/derbent">Derbent</a> to hold off incursions from the north, and a later chronicle credits him with refounding the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yazd">Yazd</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He died in 457, reportedly without naming a successor, and civil war followed between his sons. Hormizd III took the throne at Ray, while Peroz fled north-east and raised an army, and their mother, Denag, ruled temporarily as regent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesiphon">Ctesiphon</a>. Peroz emerged victorious. Persian sources generally praise Yazdegerd as an astute and well-read ruler, known to Ibn al-Balkhi and Hamza al-Isfahani as Yazdegerd the Gentle, a favourable account that is tied to a policy of persecuting non-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zoroastrianism">Zoroastrians</a>, which appeased the aristocracy and the Zoroastrian priesthood.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Yazdegerd II” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 450–451 Albanians joined the Armenian and Iberian rising against the attempt of the Sasanian king <b>Yazdegerd II</b> to impose Zoroastrianism on the Christian Caucasus, a rising crushed at the Battle of Avarayr in 451.</blockquote><a href="/empire/caucasian-albania">Caucasian Albania · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Persian sources name them as foes of <b>Yazdegerd II</b> from 442.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>On the Avarayr plain, the army of the Sasanian king <b>Yazdegerd II</b>, who sought to impose Zoroastrianism, defeated Armenian rebels led by Vardan Mamikonian, who was killed.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-26">26 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>They fought repeated wars with Sasanian kings such as <b>Yazdegerd II</b> and Peroz I, and probably clashed with the Gupta Empire in northern India.</blockquote><a href="/state/kidarite-kingdom">Kidarite Kingdom</a></li><li><blockquote>When <b>Yazdegerd II</b> tried to impose Zoroastrianism, Vardan Mamikonian led a revolt that was crushed at Avarayr in 451 but secured toleration, confirmed by the Treaty of Nvarsak in 484.</blockquote><a href="/state/persarmenia">Persarmenia</a></li></ul></section>
